WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.020
Before women were asking “am I a Carrie
or a Samantha?”, they were asking “Am

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:11.249
I a Jo or an Amy?” Before there was Edward
vs Jacob, there was Laurie vs Professor Bhaer.

00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:15.559
And over the more than 150 years since Little
Women was originally published, there have

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:20.550
been dozens of adaptations,
feature films, television adaptations, plays,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.980
ballets, operas and at least two animes based
on it.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.270
So despite being written off as proto-chick
lit or kiddie lit or as Alcott herself said,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.450
"moral pap for the young,” Little Women
has worked its way into the consciousness

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.540
of readers for the last 150 years, and has
stayed there.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.380
But what is it about the tale of the March
sisters that keeps us coming back?

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.040
For those of you haven’t read the book or
seen one of these many adaptations, Little

00:00:49.041 --> 00:00:54.850
Women, written by Louisa May Alcott and published
in two parts between 1868 and 1869, is the

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.579
story of the four March sisters, Meg, Jo,
Beth and Amy. The novel follows their trials

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.250
and tribulations growing up in Concord, Massachusetts
during the Civil War, while their father is

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.710
off serving as a chaplain in the Union Army.
They are brought up by their austere and wise

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:14.100
mother, or Marmee, and are taught all sorts
of empowering lessons about being kind, charitable,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.210
intelligent, and loving in the face of hardship.
The token boy of their world is their rich,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.890
half-Italian orphan friend Laurie, who is
our romantic McGuffin for the novel.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:26.340
Now in terms of plot, the book is less story
driven and more of a series of character vignettes

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.310
exploring the lives of the titular “Little
Women” as they leave behind childhood and

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.610
enter adulthood. Giving away their Christmas
breakfast, Jo cutting of her hair for money,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:40.479
Amy burning Jo’s manuscript (which has scarred
generations of young female writers forever),

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.890
Laurie’s proposal to Jo and her rejection
of it, and Laurie eventually settling for

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.229
Amy. And what else? Oh, yeah. Beth dying tragically young.

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:50.500
Spoiler alert, I guess.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.170
Now you might think that all this mundane,
sentimental, and moral lesson rearing was

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.090
the kind of work that Alcott was used to writing,
but if you take a look at how the novel came

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.200
to be, you find that Little Women was actually
somewhat outside of her wheelhouse.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.270
In September of 1867, publisher Thomas Niles
wrote to Alcott to ask if she would write

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.959
a book specifically targeted to young girls,
but she wasn’t initially wild about the

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:17.980
idea. Said Alcott: “Never liked girls or
knew many, except my sisters, but our queer

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.530
plays and experiences may prove interesting,
though I doubt it." Ouch. However, in spite

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.670
of her initial animosity towards the idea,
Alcott took the experiences of her life and

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.170
family and, with some creative flair, turned
them into what would become Little Women.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.560
According to Anne Boyd Rioux, Alcott used
the book as an “opportunity to immortalize

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.870
some aspects of her life while concealing
others.”

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.860
Alcott was herself the second of four sisters,
the others being Abigail May, Elizabeth, and

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:51.650
Anna. Roughly, Anna became Meg, Abigail May
became Amy, and Elizabeth was … well Beth.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.180
And Jo was basically a self-insert of Alcott.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.080
The Alcott patriarch, Amos Bronson Alcott
was both a man very ahead of his time in terms

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.200
of abolition, women’s rights, and education,
while also being a fanatic who put his principles

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.460
ahead of his family’s well being. Because
he was vegan, before anyone thought veganism

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.860
was cool, his family wasn’t allowed to eat
meat or wear anything other than linen. In

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:18.459
the winter. In Concord, Massachusetts. And
he only approved of vegetables that grew upward.

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:21.960
So potatoes were out, too. Oh, and he left
his wife while she was pregnant with their

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.300
third child to go stay in a room by the Pennsylvania
library so he could read.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.150
Maybe this constant absence in his family’s
life is why Mr. March isn’t around in the

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:35.690
novel, although Alcott gives the fictional
father a more noble reason to be gone.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.280
Thanks, Daddy-A.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.480
As the scholar Judith Fetterley writes, “Much
of the popularity of Little Women, then and

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.150
now, derives from its embodiment of the cultural
fantasy of the happy family,” which the

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.410
Alcotts both were and were not.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.580
The Alcott sisters like the March sisters,
had real artistic ambitions. The sister who

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.640
Meg is based on had dreamed of being an actress,
“just as Louisa had dreamed of becoming

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.420
a famous author and May a famous artist.”
And with Jo, Alcott’s stand-in, the shared

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.310
dream to be a famous writer blurs the lines
between reality and fiction.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.599
Alcott wrote primarily as a means to provide
for her family -- according to Rioux, “writing

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:13.980
could never be a purely artistic affair. There
was room in the Alcott family for only one

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.579
self-absorbed genius.” Thanks, Daddy-A.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:22.789
Alcott would get between $50 and $75 for each
of her stories, which was pretty bank for

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:27.020
the day--she actually preferred to write her
revenge and saucy stuff under the pen name

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.210
A. M. Barnard, to keep the Alcott #brand consistent.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.270
While Jo at first writes saucy, violent short
stories out of general love of the game, she

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.600
ends up doing it because it makes money, and
because she has yet to write her real masterpiece,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.979
her in-universe novel also titled Little Women.

00:04:42.979 --> 00:04:46.460
Alcott successfully negotiated with her publishers
so that she kept the copyright for Little

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:50.400
Women, which they caved on with little pressure
because they didn’t think it would end up

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.819
being the gangbuster that it was. Part One’s
initial printing sold out so quickly that

00:04:54.819 --> 00:04:59.389
the company who published it, Roberts Brothers,
had trouble keeping up with demand! And it’s

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:01.319
never been out of print since.

00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:05.740
And little women wasn’t just a hit with,
well, little women. As a contemporary of Alcott’s

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.690
wrote, “Grave merchants and lawyers meeting
on their way down town in the morning said

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:14.259
to each other, Have you read little women
and laughed as they said it.”

00:05:14.259 --> 00:05:18.330
But more noteworthy than its commercial success
was what Little Women brought to the table

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:23.310
on the literary side of things. According
to Rioux, “What Alcott brought to children’s—and

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:27.669
American—literature was the fresh language
of American colloquialism, [...] and a precursor

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:31.990
to the even more “vulgar” speech of the
lower-class Huck Finn, who would appear sixteen

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:37.410
years later, in 1884.” In terms of writing,
what made Alcott’s work so noticeable was

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:41.879
the way it so realistically captured the slang
and “vulgarity” of everyday life without

00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:43.560
wordy purple prose.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.639
But if the book allowed Alcott to comfortably
provide for her family, it also meant that

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:51.689
she had to deal with... fans.

00:05:51.689 --> 00:05:54.860
According to Rioux: “Their letters poured
in with demands to know what would happen

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.599
to the four March girls, or, most importantly,
whom they would marry, ‘as if that was the

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:02.830
only end and aim of a woman’s life,’ the
author grumbled.”

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:07.539
Once the second volume was published, a lot
of fans and critics, both back then and today,

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:11.569
took real issue with Jo giving up her career
to be married, but that wasn’t where Alcott

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:15.860
had originally intended to take the character
-- it was a narrative choice made at the encouragement

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.449
of Alcott’s publishers. Alcott wanted Jo
to be an unmarried successful writer, but

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:22.715
that idea was a little too revolutionary for
the 1860’s.

00:06:23.899 --> 00:06:28.250
As the scholar Ann Douglas has noted, Alcott
“could not disregard, however, the unpleasant

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:33.639
facts confronting her female contemporaries
and characters; unlike her male peers, a Victorian

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:37.469
woman rarely had a spouse, a family, and a
full-time career.”

00:06:37.469 --> 00:06:48.679
SO Jo, under pressure from the publisher,
had to have a husband and not a career. But

00:06:48.679 --> 00:06:53.979
if Jo was gonna have a happy ending, it was
going to be in terms that felt real. Alcott,

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:58.149
much like Rian Johnson, didn’t believe in
giving in to fanservice. Despite the reader’s

00:06:58.149 --> 00:07:02.310
wishes, Louisa refused to pair Jo up with
Laurie, her dreamboat neighbor with enough

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:07.099
money to solve all of her family’s problems.
Denying 19th century readers their ultimate

00:07:07.099 --> 00:07:09.300
OTP.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:15.469
So she gave Jo her very own creepy old guy--I
mean, older German professor--and the film

00:07:15.469 --> 00:07:19.199
adaptations have been trying to figure out
how to make THAT work for over a hundred years.

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:24.659
(Newer versions deal with it by making him
either a) hot and/or b) younger)

00:07:24.659 --> 00:07:28.439
So even though the publisher wanted the picture
of mid 19th century womanhood filtered through

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:33.360
a certain lens, Alcott’s personal life is
heavily infused into the final product - and

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.599
that sense of realness is a part of what has
made it so enduring. Douglas has called these

00:07:37.599 --> 00:07:42.139
sisters, “the only mythic American characters
from the Victorian era unashamedly believably

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:45.739
actively capable of love friendship, loyalty
and sheer fun.”

00:07:45.739 --> 00:07:49.789
Rioux adds that “Little Women was a cultural
phenomenon that knew no boundaries of age,

00:07:49.789 --> 00:07:51.120
gender, or class.”

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.809
Yeah, so let’s unpack that.

00:07:54.809 --> 00:08:00.879
It is interesting to think that a book about
four cis-hetero white women has managed to

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:05.150
last so long and be so universally loved.
From the queer readings of Jo and the racebent

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:09.830
adaptations or books inspired by Little Women,
it is clear that there is something that connects

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:14.300
women across the board with this novel. It’s
more than just a book about a group of sisters

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.839
coming of age and getting married - And perhaps
it’s that the March sisters have the relative

00:08:18.839 --> 00:08:23.259
freedom to be themselves and feel cherished,
despite society’s constraints.

00:08:23.259 --> 00:08:28.729
They feel human and tangible with their virtues
and vices, while also having the full ability

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:34.240
to experience girlhood. For a lot of marginalized
people, the girlhood of Meg, Jo, Beth, and

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:40.019
Amy might not be relatable, but it is as idyllic,
fantastic, and magical as the idea of getting

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:44.480
a letter from Hogwarts. For Alcott, the cozy
world of the Marches was somewhat distant

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.430
from her own life of poverty, abolition, and
frustration.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.600
But there’s always a need to return to a
place of innocence and freedom, regardless

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:57.170
if it is something you’ve experienced or
not. as times change, we find new ways to

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:00.089
express the universal elements in Little Women.

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:03.310
...and i’ll let you finish

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.850
Louisa May Alcott was raised to believe she
could be anything she wanted to be, that there

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:11.220
were no limits in what women could do, in
spite of real world limitations. Despite her

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.190
initial concerns, that comes through on the
page. Little Women isn’t important because

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:19.510
it is “realistic” or a perfect mirror
of our world. It is important because it inspired

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:22.970
generations of women to be whole human beings.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.459
That is worth revisiting.

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:29.750
Although, you know … maybe throw in some
adaptations of Zora Neale Hurston, Toni Morrison,

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:42.749
Amy Tan, and Isabel Allende in there for seasoning.

